      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e I suppose in practice there should be no overhead if we make them non-inlined: the compiler will still inline those \"instances\" of these functions that it considers worth inlining (even without LTO).\n\nMy understanding on this: I think if the function is in the same file, then compiler makes a judgement about inlining or not. if the function is in another file, the compiler cannot see the implementation(I think C compiler takes one file at a time for processing and does not cross reference) and hence generates code to make a function call and leaves it to linker. This is just my understanding based on some reading, but not verified in practice.",
